1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,389
Watch parallel rays pass through two curved

2
00:00:03,429 --> 00:00:06,555
glass surfaces and crowd toward a common focus.

3
00:00:06,625 --> 00:00:10,053
Now zoom in. The outer rays cross before the central rays,

4
00:00:10,093 --> 00:00:12,416
so one point cannot catch them all.

5
00:00:13,286 --> 00:00:15,307
How can we predict this mismatch without

6
00:00:15,347 --> 00:00:18,379
pretending a real lens is infinitely thin?

7
00:00:18,449 --> 00:00:20,670
Trace each ray: find the next surface,

8
00:00:20,710 --> 00:00:22,873
change its direction, then repeat.

9
00:00:23,693 --> 00:00:26,828
A ray marks a local direction of light travel.

10
00:00:26,868 --> 00:00:28,969
It is not the complete electric field.

11
00:00:29,039 --> 00:00:32,116
Store a starting position and a unit direction vector.

12
00:00:32,156 --> 00:00:35,207
Distance along that direction is our variable.

13
00:00:35,277 --> 00:00:38,709
In uniform material, a ray follows a straight line.

14
00:00:38,749 --> 00:00:40,879
A boundary can change its direction.

15
00:00:40,949 --> 00:00:42,610
Our lens has two spherical surfaces,

16
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:46,955
four millimetres apart, with glass index one point five.

17
00:00:47,025 --> 00:00:50,938
You see a central slice of a three-dimensional calculation.

18
00:00:50,978 --> 00:00:54,146
The optical axis runs horizontally, along z.

19
00:00:54,216 --> 00:00:58,018
Curvature, thickness, and aperture specify the glass.

20
00:00:58,058 --> 00:01:00,234
We still need to calculate where light goes.

21
00:01:01,054 --> 00:01:03,908
First, extend the incoming line until it meets the

22
00:01:03,948 --> 00:01:07,341
first spherical surface. Calculate that intersection.

23
00:01:07,411 --> 00:01:09,432
Substitute the ray into the sphere equation.

24
00:01:09,472 --> 00:01:13,503
A quadratic gives two possible roots, one, or none.

25
00:01:13,573 --> 00:01:16,348
But our optical surface is only a spherical cap.

26
00:01:16,388 --> 00:01:19,647
A root on the opposite cap must be rejected.

27
00:01:20,517 --> 00:01:23,024
Keep a forward intersection on the correct cap,

28
00:01:23,064 --> 00:01:27,069
inside the clear aperture. Otherwise, report a missed surface.

29
00:01:27,139 --> 00:01:29,129
At the accepted point, construct the normal.

30
00:01:29,169 --> 00:01:32,769
For a sphere, it follows the line through its centre.

31
00:01:32,839 --> 00:01:34,827
Orient the normal into the next medium.

32
00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:39,690
Measure bending from this local direction, not from the optical axis.

33
00:01:40,510 --> 00:01:42,653
Split the incoming unit direction into

34
00:01:42,693 --> 00:01:45,518
parts tangent and normal to the interface.

35
00:01:45,588 --> 00:01:49,932
The phase variation along that interface must match on both sides.

36
00:01:49,972 --> 00:01:52,148
That determines the tangential part.

37
00:01:52,218 --> 00:01:55,117
Divide the first refractive index by the second.

38
00:01:55,157 --> 00:01:58,730
Multiply the tangential direction by this ratio.

39
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,615
From air into glass, the ratio is below one.

40
00:02:01,655 --> 00:02:03,851
The ray bends toward the normal.

41
00:02:03,921 --> 00:02:06,620
Rebuild the normal component to make a unit vector.

42
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:09,418
Choose the branch entering the next medium.

43
00:02:10,288 --> 00:02:12,019
This is vector Snell refraction.

44
00:02:12,059 --> 00:02:16,387
The familiar sine law describes the same rule using angles.

45
00:02:16,457 --> 00:02:19,330
From glass into air, the tangential part grows.

46
00:02:19,370 --> 00:02:23,857
Beyond the critical angle, no real transmitted direction exists.

47
00:02:23,927 --> 00:02:27,770
That is total internal reflection. Our transmitted path stops;

48
00:02:27,810 --> 00:02:31,449
following the reflected branch requires another trace.

49
00:02:32,269 --> 00:02:35,476
Find the second surface using the new direction.

50
00:02:35,516 --> 00:02:38,226
A thick lens requires two separate refractions.

51
00:02:38,296 --> 00:02:41,735
Recompute the normal and exchange the refractive indices.

52
00:02:41,775 --> 00:02:44,583
This time, the ray leaves glass for air.

53
00:02:44,653 --> 00:02:47,282
Intersect the outgoing line with a detector plane.

54
00:02:47,322 --> 00:02:50,917
This point is the ray's predicted landing position.

55
00:02:50,987 --> 00:02:52,542
Repeat across the entrance aperture.

56
00:02:52,582 --> 00:02:57,631
Every path shown comes from surface intersections and Snell refraction.

57
00:02:57,701 --> 00:03:00,149
Near the axis, ray angles are small.

58
00:03:00,189 --> 00:03:04,295
Linearizing their motion gives the paraxial focus, marked here.

59
00:03:04,365 --> 00:03:06,633
That focus is a useful reference,

60
00:03:06,673 --> 00:03:09,602
not a promise for every ray through a finite lens.

61
00:03:10,422 --> 00:03:12,876
Open the aperture without changing the lens.

62
00:03:12,916 --> 00:03:16,437
Outer rays now sample steeper surface regions.

63
00:03:16,507 --> 00:03:19,654
Watch the enlarged focus. For this spherical lens,

64
00:03:19,694 --> 00:03:22,988
outer rays cross the axis before central rays.

65
00:03:23,858 --> 00:03:26,133
This mismatch is spherical aberration.

66
00:03:26,173 --> 00:03:29,950
It comes from the geometry, not a coarse numerical grid.

67
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:32,977
Move the detector. The bundle narrows and spreads,

68
00:03:33,017 --> 00:03:34,859
but its rays never all meet.

69
00:03:34,929 --> 00:03:38,261
Here, best focus minimizes the root mean square distance

70
00:03:38,301 --> 00:03:41,895
from the spot centroid. Other criteria can differ.

71
00:03:41,965 --> 00:03:44,771
Tilt the input. The off-axis pattern changes,

72
00:03:44,811 --> 00:03:48,316
so an on-axis test cannot describe the whole image.

73
00:03:48,386 --> 00:03:52,251
Closing the aperture reduces geometric spread but rejects light.

74
00:03:52,291 --> 00:03:55,700
A smaller ray spot is not the whole story.

75
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:59,377
These are geometric paths. Their landing points

76
00:03:59,417 --> 00:04:01,908
are not a diffraction point spread function.

77
00:04:01,978 --> 00:04:06,156
Waves still spread through a finite aperture, as in episode three.

78
00:04:06,196 --> 00:04:09,321
Closing the aperture can increase diffraction blur.

79
00:04:10,191 --> 00:04:14,700
Our ideal surfaces use one fixed index. We omit reflected power,

80
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:18,167
dispersion, absorption, and manufacturing errors.

81
00:04:18,237 --> 00:04:22,177
A missed surface or total internal reflection is a tracing outcome.

82
00:04:22,217 --> 00:04:25,263
Check those before comparing image errors.

83
00:04:25,333 --> 00:04:27,651
Other surface shapes use the same logic.

84
00:04:27,691 --> 00:04:30,800
More rays sample the chosen model more densely;

85
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:32,997
they do not add missing physics.

86
00:04:33,817 --> 00:04:36,467
One ray follows one recipe: intersect,

87
00:04:36,507 --> 00:04:39,522
construct the normal, refract, and continue.

88
00:04:39,592 --> 00:04:43,324
A bundle reveals the consequences. Aperture, field angle,

89
00:04:43,364 --> 00:04:47,022
and detector position all affect the geometric image.

90
00:04:47,092 --> 00:04:49,417
The paraxial focus starts the design.

91
00:04:49,457 --> 00:04:54,013
Exact surface tracing tests the finite lens within its assumptions.

92
00:04:54,083 --> 00:04:56,813
Next, we will choose lens shapes using image error,

93
00:04:56,853 --> 00:04:59,192
while keeping the required focal length.
